In information geometry one studies smooth families of probability distributions equipped with additional structure (a Riemannian metric, dual affine connections, and a divergence). Classically, a divergence on a smooth manifold M is a smooth function D: M × M → ℝ satisfying:
(i) D(p, q) ≥ 0 for all p, q ∈ M, with equality iff p = q (positivity / non-degeneracy);
(ii) the Hessian of D(p, q) along the diagonal {p = q}, taken with respect to q, induces a positive-definite Riemannian metric g on M (so g_{ij}(p) = ∂²D(p,q)/∂q^i∂q^j |_{q=p});
(iii) the third derivatives of D produce a torsion-free affine connection ∇ and its dual ∇* with respect to g.

Standard examples include the Kullback–Leibler divergence on a statistical manifold and Bregman divergences associated to strictly convex functions.

However, two natural extensions of the framework break the classical axioms:
(a) the geometry of dual connections (∇, ∇*, g) on a manifold can be developed without requiring D to be positive;
(b) the geometry of singular statistical models (where the Fisher information may degenerate) does not require that the Riemannian metric be obtained as the Hessian of D along the diagonal.

Problem. Formulate a definition of "divergence function" on a (possibly singular) smooth manifold M that:
- specializes to KL-divergence and Bregman divergence in the classical regular case;
- accommodates dual-connection geometries in which positivity (axiom (i)) need not hold;
- accommodates singular statistical models in which the Riemannian (or pseudo-Riemannian, or degenerate) metric structure is not required to arise as the Hessian of the divergence on the diagonal (axiom (ii));
- still canonically determines, in the regular case, the induced metric and the pair of dual affine connections, and behaves functorially under appropriate morphisms (e.g. sufficient statistics / smooth embeddings of statistical models).

Give such a definition and verify these compatibility properties, or prove that no definition satisfying all of the above requirements can exist.

